       The  mmkkddiirraatt() system call operates in exactly the same way as mmkkddiirr(),
       except for the differences described here.

       If _p_a_t_h is relative, then it is interpreted relative to  the  directory
       referred  to  by the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d (rather than relative to the
       current working directory of the calling process, as is done by mmkkddiirr()
       for a relative pathname).

       If _p_a_t_h is relative and _d_i_r_f_d is the special value AATT__FFDDCCWWDD, then  _p_a_t_h
       is interpreted relative to the current working directory of the calling
       process (like mmkkddiirr()).

       If _p_a_t_h is absolute, then _d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       See ooppeennaatt(2) for an explanation of the need for mmkkddiirraatt().

